<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jan Smuts</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1870">1870</a>–<a href="/years/1950">1950</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jan Smuts was a South African statesman who fought as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> general and then, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-botha">Louis Botha</a>, led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/het-volk">Het Volk</a> party in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> Colony and negotiated union in 1910. He was prime minister of the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> in 1919–1924 and 1939–1948, served in the British war cabinets and helped found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jan-smuts.jpg" alt="Jan Smuts" width="320" height="452" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown - Toesprake deur sy hoog-edeagbare JC Smuts · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Genl_JC_Smuts.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jan Christiaan Smuts was born on 24 May 1870 at the family farm, Bovenplaats, near Malmesbury in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>, to prosperous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaner</a> parents. As the second son he was expected to work on the farm, but when his elder brother died in 1882 he was sent to school in his place. He caught up with his contemporaries within four years, entered Victoria College, Stellenbosch, in 1886 and went on to study law at Christ's College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, on a scholarship. He was called to the bar at the Middle Temple in 1894 and returned home the following year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Smuts first supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cecil-rhodes">Cecil Rhodes</a>, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jameson-raid">Jameson Raid</a> of 1895-96 outraged him, and he became state attorney of the South African Republic in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a> he served first as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-kruger">Paul Kruger</a>'s aide in Pretoria and from mid-1900 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koos-de-la-rey">Koos de la Rey</a> in the Western Transvaal, where he excelled at hit-and-run warfare. He helped negotiate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-vereeniging">Treaty of Vereeniging</a> in 1902. In May 1904 he joined other former Transvaal generals in founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/het-volk">Het Volk</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-botha">Louis Botha</a> as leader and himself as deputy. After the fall of the Conservative government in December 1905 he and Botha went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and won responsible government for the Transvaal.</p><p>In the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> of 1910 Botha became prime minister and Smuts held the ministries of the Interior, Mines and Defence. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-defence-force-south-africa">Union Defence Force</a>, put down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maritz-rebellion">Maritz Rebellion</a>, helped conquer German South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> and in 1916 took charge of the campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-east-africa">German East Africa</a>. He sat in the Imperial War Cabinet from 1917 and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920">Paris Peace Conference</a> helped shape the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a>. He succeeded Botha as prime minister in 1919 and lost office in 1924 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/j-b-m-hertzog">J. B. M. Hertzog</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Smuts returned as deputy prime minister in a coalition with Hertzog, and when Hertzog's motion for neutrality was defeated by 80 votes to 67 in 1939 he became prime minister again and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. He was made a field marshal on 24 May 1941. He wrote the first draft of the preamble to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-charter">United Nations Charter</a> and signed the charters of both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>. His book Holism and Evolution (1926) set out the concept of holism. He supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/racial-segregation">racial segregation</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a> won the 1948 election, after which apartheid was introduced.</p><p>In 1948 he became Chancellor of the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>. He suffered a coronary thrombosis on 29 May 1950 and died of a heart attack at his farm, Doornkloof, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a>, on 11 September 1950.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jan Smuts” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Men formed in the republic’s service, among them Louis Botha and <b>Jan Smuts</b>, went on to lead the Union of South Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/south-african-republic">South African Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>By the Treaty of Vereeniging in 1902 the two Boer republics became British colonies, and in 1906–1907 they received responsible government, which brought the Afrikaner generals Louis Botha and <b>Jan Smuts</b> to power in the Transvaal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/union-of-south-africa">Union of South Africa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Self-government came in 1907, and the Het Volk party of Louis Botha and <b>Jan Smuts</b> won the election.</blockquote><a href="/state/transvaal-colony">Transvaal Colony</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Riebeek West, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. Died in Irene, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-33.35,18.87&amp;year=1922" aria-label="Riebeek West on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5032 3374 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jan Smuts"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5482 3644h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5482 3644h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-33.35,18.87&amp;year=1922">What was here in 1922? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
